<p>I&rsquo;ve talked about journaling and its importance many times in this blog, but after not journaling for a while I have even more justification for it. At its core, journaling is where my brain grows and gets nourished. Without it, I&rsquo;m just a dot in time, floating between past and future.</p>
<p>I don&rsquo;t mean to sound depressing, but think about it. Experiencing life is important, but if you don&rsquo;t reflect on it, you can&rsquo;t fully grasp its meaning to you. You can be happy or sad, you can even learn a thing or two, but without having these experiences in front of you for a second pass, where you sit and connect the dots, how much of it can you really retain? If you&rsquo;re new to journaling or considering it, I suggest you also look into one of my posts <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/02/07/lessons-about-journalling-and-taking.html">explaining its importance</a>.</p>
<p>But let&rsquo;s drop the philosophical coffee rant for a minute. What I want to get to is my process, after I realized I hadn&rsquo;t journaled for a while, and the guides I set for myself to help with it.</p>
<p>My tools and techniques may be different from yours, so treat those with the personal flexibility you need. OK, here we go:</p>
<ol>
<li>Aim to create a journal entry every day. Even if it&rsquo;s just a list of events in bullet points.</li>
<li>Expand on past events:
<ol>
<li>Reflect on each bullet point with a few comments of importance. If you feel you&rsquo;re starting to ramble, be aware of the rest of the list — you can always come back later<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup> and keep going.</li>
<li>As you go through those comments, try to add the times you did certain things. I use Emacs, so there&rsquo;s a timestamp structure (a timestamp is a date, followed by a time). It&rsquo;s a good habit to keep, and it will become useful later.</li>
<li>As your comments expand (or if you revisit later), create additional headings for them when you feel they are detailed enough to merit their own cognitive block.</li>
</ol>
</li>
<li>Look back at your day and even your week, if you wrote it down. Your quick bullet points<sup id="fnref:2"><a href="#fn:2" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">2</a></sup> should give you an idea of what you did each day. Is there something you remember now? Is there an additional meaning to something that happened a couple of days ago? Write it down. Reflecting on events like this is exactly what we&rsquo;re doing here.</li>
</ol>
<p>A bit of a ramble for Emacs folks (feel free to skip if you&rsquo;re not one of those):</p>
<p>In Emacs, I also create past events I missed so they show up in my agenda. For example, let&rsquo;s say I went grocery shopping and made a quick note about it in <a href="https://taonaw.com/archive/?q=journelly">Journelly</a>. In the evening, when I reflect and write my bullet points and pull my Journelly entries into the main journal (I might expand on this again), I also go to my events org file and write something like &ldquo;grocery shopping @ Dan&rsquo;s Fresh Market&rdquo; along with an approximate time range I was there. The event file is included in the agenda (journal entries are not), and the timestamp shows me when I was there. That&rsquo;s my quick reflection on what I did and where, when I want it in the future. Since I have the date in front of me, it&rsquo;s also easy to find the journal entry for that day, since it&rsquo;s reflected in the file name.</p>
<h3 id="what-should-be-included">What should be included?</h3>
<p>The above is a nice guide on <em>how</em> to write, but it doesn&rsquo;t tell you <em>what</em> to write. Here is the other part of my quick self-guide. This is more a list of hints that should trigger the &ldquo;hey, you might want to write about this&rdquo; bulb in my head, so once again, change it so it fits your brain bulbs:</p>
<ul>
<li>If it&rsquo;s happening somewhere else (location-based)</li>
<li>If it&rsquo;s happening with someone else (person-based)</li>
<li>If it&rsquo;s something you want to remember (could be developed into a note or a blog post)</li>
</ul>
<p>In general, this works well. If I leave home and go somewhere, it usually means I want to note it — the same goes when I do something with someone else, even if it&rsquo;s a dinner at home. The idea to keep in mind is how unusual it is, or whether something different or important happened. As you can see, I left myself a comment that if it&rsquo;s something I want to remember, I should note it (<a href="https://taonaw.com/archive/?q=denote">denote</a> in my case) or turn it into a blog post, as I&rsquo;m doing right now. Yep, this post is also a journal entry.</p>
<p>Emacs and org-mode are my essential tools when it comes to writing, and this blog is full of posts explaining how I write down things, but I wanted to make this post valuable for other folks as well. Still, I want to emphasize one final note: because Emacs is the best writing tool I have ever encountered, it also means I write more than I ever did before, which in turn means the benefits I mentioned at the start happen on a level that wouldn&rsquo;t be possible with another tool (at least, not the way I see it). The point is, use something that works for you. Your baseline should be something you don&rsquo;t think about much, like a reflex. Whether it&rsquo;s a pocket notebook, quick self-recordings on your phone, or a text file saved somewhere, whatever it is: use it well. Use it often.</p>
